# Local patches carried on top of the vendored checkout.
#
# Apply from inside the checkout after replacing it with a newer upstream tree:
#
#     cd subagents/raven-code/Raven-main && git apply ../local-patches.diff
#
# Then confirm the tree differs from upstream in exactly these files and no
# others. An empty diff-vs-upstream means a patch was lost; extra files mean
# something local crept in unrecorded.
#
# 1. everos timestamp coercion. EverOS's MessageItemDTO declares
#    `timestamp: int` (ms epoch) while raven stamps its own records with an
#    ISO-8601 string, so a caller that forwards a persisted message fails the
#    whole write with 422 INVALID_INPUT, logged at warning level and reported
#    nowhere else -- the store just stops receiving turns. Upstream still has
#    the bare `m.get("timestamp") or now_ms` as of e16aec5f.
#
#    Status: defense-in-depth, NOT an observed outage. Verified 2026-08-17 that
#    no current caller sends an ISO string (the assembler's messages carry no
#    timestamp key; the importer converts to ms before storing). Keep it for
#    the replay / direct-chat paths that do forward persisted messages.
#
# 2. in-workspace session migration removed. This build still carries
#    `SessionManager._migrate_in_workspace_sessions`, which treats
#    `<workspace>/sessions` as a legacy location and moves every file out of it
#    before `rmdir`-ing the tree. Trunk has since made `<workspace>/sessions`
#    the canonical home of the host's live transcripts, and this fork's
#    workspace defaults to `~/.raven/workspace` -- the host's own agent home --
#    so the migration fired on the host's store, not on a legacy one.
#
#    Status: observed outage, 2026-08-27. Two launches (02:39 and 02:58) each
#    relocated the whole host session tree into
#    `<STATE_ROOT>/sessions/_root_.raven_workspace/`. A conversation ended up
#    split across two roots, sub-agent records opened before a launch could not
#    be finished (`SpawnRecord.finish` returns early when its directory is
#    gone, so no `out.md`, `status` stuck at `running`, and no instance log at
#    all), and the `dest.exists()` branch `unlink`-ed one segment outright.
#    Drop the migration rather than re-point it: this fork has no legacy tree
#    of its own to migrate, so there is nothing left for it to do.
#
#    Covers `tests/test_session_state_dir.py` as well as the module. The two
#    upstream tests there assert the migration happens, so a replay carrying the
#    module hunk alone restores the fix and a suite that contradicts it (2 failed,
#    3 passed) -- and loses the regression guard this patch exists to keep. They
#    are inverted rather than deleted: the hazard is the claim, not the identifier.
